  "findings": [
    {
      "finding": "Level 2 attributes to the paper a specific claim that is not in the source (provenance check failed: quote not found). The statement — 'The paper shows that vaccinating every newborn would eliminate tuberculosis within ten years' — is a teaching choice, not a paper finding, and it is misaligned with the level's topic (treatment and the threshold).",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 2,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Replace the false attribution with a paper-supported sensitivity result, e.g. 'The paper's sensitivity analysis shows the treatment rate δ has a negative index, so increasing treatment lowers TB spread.' Change the What-if to vary the treatment rate while watching infectious, so the level teaches the paper's threshold/reproduction finding rather than an invented vaccination claim."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 3's statement is worded far more strongly than its measurement. The check holds but the measured difference is only about 6 more exposed people at year 10; the claim that 'the epidemic doubles in size and the disease can never be controlled' overstates the result.",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 3,
      "severity": "advice",
      "suggestion": "Soften the statement to match the measurement, e.g. 'With twice-as-fast progression, the exposed count is about 6 higher at year 10,' removing the unsupported 'doubles' and 'never controlled' language."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 4 is definitional: a larger recovery-rate inflow directly produces more recovered individuals, so the level teaches nothing the paper discovered. Its check also FAILS (9/20). A failed check stays failed; I do not call it acceptable.",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 4,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Replace the definitional recovery→recovered setup with a test of a paper finding: change the slider to treatment rate, watch infectious, and use What-if 'what if treatment were twice as fast?' with statement 'the infectious peak is lower by more than 100,000 people.' This teaches the paper's result that treatment reduces TB rather than a direct-flow triviality."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 5 is definitional: setting the treatment rate to zero empties the treated compartment (a compartment fed by nothing stays empty). It therefore teaches no paper finding, even though the check passes.",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 5,
      "severity": "blocking",
      "suggestion": "Change the watched variable to infectious, keep the slider as treatment rate, and set the What-if to 'what if no one were treated?' with statement 'the infectious population is substantially larger.' This tests the paper's finding that treatment lowers TB burden instead of demonstrating an empty-compartment triviality."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Level 2 also contains a confusing/unsupported phrase: 'vaccinating every newborn' is not specified in the paper (the paper discusses BCG generally, not universal newborn vaccination as a guaranteed elimination strategy within ten years).",
      "kind": "definitional",
      "level": 2,
      "severity": "advice",
      "suggestion": "Remove the specific 'every newborn' framing and replace with the paper's actual discussion of vaccination efficacy and sensitivity (e.g., vaccination rate π has a negative sensitivity index)."
    }
  ]
}
